All right, guys.

32
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.480
Fast fact number one.

33
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.240
Ben Franklin.

34
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:51.900
You might know him as the guy on the $100 bill or the inventor of the lightning rod or the bifocal lenses.

35
00:01:52.120 --> 00:02:00.580
Or maybe you know that he is the only founding father to sign all four documents credited with creating the United States of America.

36
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.860
That includes the Declaration of Independence, the Treaty of Paris, and the Constitution.

37
00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:11.200
But here are some other interesting facts about Benjamin Franklin.

38
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:11.760
What?

39
00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.500
He had 16 siblings.

40
00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:15.780
His poor mother.

41
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:16.360
Yeah.

42
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.240
His mother was pregnant her whole life.

43
00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.660
Her whole life she was pregnant.

44
00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.440
My grandfather was one of 11.

45
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:27.220
He was like the third to last born, and his mom named him Pliny.

46
00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:27.940
Oh, no.

47
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:32.000
And in my family, we've always been like, did that mean she was just like, I need a break?

48
00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.000
Hey, that's Pliny.

49
00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:34.680
Take a pause.

50
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.540
They could have named him Enough or Finished.

51
00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:40.540
Stay off me.

52
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:41.720
Yeah, get away.

53
00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:42.980
This is my kid.

54
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:43.440
Get away.

55
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.880
Well, Ben Franklin's father had two wives.

56
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:47.860
Oh, good.

57
00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:55.020
He had seven children with his first wife, and then he had 10 children with his second wife.

58
00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.840
Ben Franklin was the 15th child in that birth order.

59
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.520
That's still, that's a lot of snacks.

60
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:03.660
Oh, my God.

61
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:04.160
It's a lot.

62
00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:07.640
I mean, you're making a lot of, can I have some waters?

63
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:08.580
Yeah.

64
00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.360
Ben Franklin only had two years of a formal education.

65
00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:12.840
Wow.

66
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.120
He was a vegetarian.

67
00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:15.300
Oh.

68
00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.340
Like your character of Angela Martin.

69
00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.240
This might explain why you were so taken with him in this episode.

70
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:21.340
Oh, well, maybe.

71
00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:21.880
Mm-hmm.

72
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:28.980
He started the first volunteer fire station in Philadelphia, and he invented an instrument

73
00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.540
called the Glass Armonica.

74
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:32.660
That's not real.

75
00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:33.780
It's real.

76
00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.520
It was used by both Mozart and Beethoven.

77
00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:37.540
Here's what it is.

78
00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:42.100
It is basically, it is a series of glass orbs.

79
00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:42.620
Okay.

80
00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:49.680
That you play by spinning them and then getting your finger wet and putting your finger on

81
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:50.200
the orb.

82
00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.580
Oh, we've seen this.

83
00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.020
This is like in talent shows.

84
00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:53.720
Yeah.

85
00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.400
Like, seriously, a junior high talent show.

86
00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:00.920
There's like someone that sets up a table of glasses and then is like, me, me, me, me,

87
00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:01.400
me, me.

88
00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:02.080
Yeah.

89
00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.400
Ben Franklin invented that.

90
00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:04.360
Okay.

91
00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:05.340
And here's the thing.

92
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.360
He would later write about this invention.

93
00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:08.420
I loved this.

94
00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:14.300
Quote, of all of my inventions, the glass Armonica has given me the greatest personal

95
00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.080
satisfaction.

96
00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.320
More than the bifocals.

97
00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:23.020
Why do I feel like if you went over to his house, he was always busting out his glass

98
00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:26.900
harmonica and you're just like, hey, I just want to, and he's like, me, me, me, me, me.

99
00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:28.800
You're like, okay, then we get it.

100
00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:29.640
We get it.

101
00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:30.440
Exactly.

102
00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:36.880
So Ben Franklin was a prolific inventor, you know, the kite in the air and all the yada

103
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:37.320
yada.

104
00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:37.940
Yeah.

105
00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:41.260
Then he died of a disease that could have been treated by antibiotics today.

106
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.460
What did he die from?

107
00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:48.120
Well, some people think he died from complications of syphilis, but he died from a disease that

108
00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:51.220
I hadn't heard of that sounded like sort of similar to pneumonia.

109
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.440
Oh, okay.

110
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:54.480
Yeah.

111
00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.240
But is something treatable with antibiotics likely today?

112
00:04:58.600 --> 00:04:59.500
How old was he?

113
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.580
I don't know, Angela.

114
00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:02.180
I'm sorry.

115
00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:03.600
What was the name of the disease?

116
00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:04.900
How old was he?

117
00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:05.280
Okay.

118
00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:05.800
Okay.

119
00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:06.280
Okay.

120
00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:06.660
Okay.

121
00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:06.960
Okay.

122
00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.380
What's fast fact number two?

123
00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:16.360
Fast fact number two is Andy Daly, the outstanding comedic actor who played Ben Franklin in this

124
00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:16.880
episode.

125
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:17.880
Yes.

126
00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.220
Are you going to tell him what we got?

127
00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.300
We got some audio clips.

128
00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:22.860
And they're so good.

129
00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.380
He is so funny.

130
00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:25.960
Jenna and I love these, Andy.

131
00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:27.220
Thank you for sending them in.

132
00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:28.680
Here's the thing.

133
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:35.040
You might know Andy Daly from Veep, Reno 911, Modern Family, Comedy Bang Bang, Eastbound

134
00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:35.580
and Down.

135
00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.620
He studied and performed improv with the Upright Citizens Brigade.

136
00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.100
We have a very interesting little thing in common, Andy and I.

137
00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:44.860
What?

138
00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:49.660
Both of our first television credits were for Spin City.

139
00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.760
Wait, is Spin City with Charlie Sheen?

140
00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.780
Well, I did the Charlie Sheen one, but he did the Michael J.

141
00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:55.480
Fox one.

142
00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:56.160
Oh, right.

143
00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:56.940
There were two.

144
00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:57.820
Yeah.

145
00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:03.900
Well, guys, we asked Andy how he got his role in the office, and he said he got it the old

146
00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:04.560
fashion way.

147
00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:08.080
He auditioned for Allison Jones, our casting director.

148
00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.340
But he had a funny story about his audition and first meeting Allison.

149
00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:13.900
Here's what he had to say.

150
00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:14.480
Hello.

151
00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:18.580
This is Andy Daly, A-ing your cues about Ben Franklin.

152
00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.740
Here's my funny story about Allison Jones.

153
00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:27.860
When the UCB Theater opened in Los Angeles, we were doing ASCAT every Saturday and Sunday

154
00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:28.120
night.

155
00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:31.440
That's a fully improvised long-form show, long-form improv show.

156
00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:38.580
And I was in it every Saturday and Sunday, and we acquired a superfan, this woman who just

157
00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.900
seemed to be at almost every show.

158
00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:46.020
And we got to talking to her a little bit during the show and a little bit after the show, and

159
00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:51.080
she just seemed like such a sweet, nice lady who was just a real improv superfan.

160
00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:57.040
And then I got an audition for this role of Benjamin Franklin in the office, and I went

161
00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:59.740
to the audition, and the casting director was that lady.

162
00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:07.240
So I've always said that that speaks so well of Allison Jones, that she is like somebody who

163
00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:12.980
goes out to shows and is a comedy fan, and that's why she is the greatest casting director

164
00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:15.000
probably in town for comedies.

165
00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:17.760
She casts all your favorite comedies, and that's why.

166
00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:20.040
Okay, but yes, I auditioned.

167
00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:24.240
And by the way, when I got that audition for Ben Franklin, I thought, well, I'm going to

168
00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:25.680
go, because why not?

169
00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:29.380
I've got nothing better to do, but they'll never hire me to play Ben Franklin, because

170
00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:35.460
I was in my mid-30s and in the shape of my life.

171
00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.120
I mean, I'm like, I've got a swimmer's body.

172
00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:38.700
No, I don't know.

173
00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:46.260
But I figured they're going to hire somebody, some middle-aged, paunchy, bald dude, right?

174
00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:47.260
Wouldn't you think?

175
00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:50.040
But I was like, well, I'll go anyway, and I'll do my beSt.

176
00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.940
And that has happened to me with so many of the roles that I've gotten.

177
00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:58.580
I have gone just for the hell of it, even thinking they'll never hire me for this.

178
00:07:58.580 --> 00:07:59.460
I'm all wrong for it.

179
00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:01.720
And I'm so glad that I was wrong.

180
00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:04.840
It was a fun audition, and I was surprised to get the part.

181
00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:07.280
Surprised, but delighted.

182
00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:08.640
There you go.

183
00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:12.160
I love that Allison Jones was just going to all of those shows.

184
00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:13.240
I just love it.

185
00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:14.720
I think she still does.

186
00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.100
And all of you aspiring actors out there, go to every audition.

187
00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:19.820
You don't know what they're looking for.

188
00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:21.100
Why not you?

189
00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:22.620
That's what I always say.

190
00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:23.940
Yeah, why not you?

191
00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.140
Don't psych yourself out of a part.

192
00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:26.860
Yeah.

193
00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:28.200
Well, I love that.

194
00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:29.360
What's your Fast Fact No.

195
00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:29.680
3?

196
00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:32.020
Well, before we move on to Fast Fact No.

197
00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.820
3, we have a fan question from Morgan McLeod.

198
00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.120
I need to know more about Elizabeth the Stripper.

199
00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:42.480
She is amazing, and I love how she reprises her role in a few more episodes.

200
00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:45.020
How did she get cast for this role?

201
00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.440
Well, guys, her name is Jackie DeBotten.

202
00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:50.460
She is so funny.

203
00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:51.680
She's so funny.

204
00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:53.080
She's so perfect.

205
00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:54.120
Oh, my God.

206
00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.340
Well, we tried to get in touch with her.

207
00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:56.800
We did.

208
00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.520
Because we really wanted Jackie to send in audio clips, too.

209
00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:03.840
But Jackie, I don't think you check your Instagram very often.

210
00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:08.940
Because the next time you go on there, you're going to have a sweet DM from Ms. Angela Kinsey

211
00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.440
just waiting for you.

212
00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:11.780
Well, listen, Jackie.

213
00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:16.400
Someday you're going to see that DM from me, and you have two more episodes, and we would

214
00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:18.060
love to talk to you.

215
00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:19.540
So hit us up.

216
00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:20.820
Yes.

217
00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:24.080
Jackie comes back for Fun Run and the finale.

218
00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:30.460
I reached out to Allison Jones about Jackie, and she told me that Jackie auditioned for the

219
00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:34.680
role in Los Angeles was fantastic, was kind of known in the comedy circles here.

220
00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:38.620
She has since moved to New York and still works a bunch there.

221
00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:41.200
So that's what she's up to.

222
00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:45.420
If you're friends with Jackie DeBotten and you hear this, tell her to check her DMs.

223
00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:46.040
Yeah.

224
00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:47.260
Tell her to hit me up.

225
00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:47.800
All right.

226
00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:50.000
Now, fast fact number three.

227
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:57.400
Fast fact number three is that we shot this episode in January, and it was our first episode

228
00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:01.420
back after taking a very long holiday break hiatus.

229
00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:08.680
And over that break, Angela, do you remember we got nominated for our first Screen Actors

230
00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:09.400
Guild Award?

231
00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:11.480
I remember we were so excited.

232
00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.080
Guys, here's the thing about the Screen Actors Guild Awards.

233
00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:18.860
It's actors nominating other actors for their work, their body of work.

234
00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.440
And if you win as a cast, everyone gets an award.

235
00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.460
That is not the case with the Emmys.

236
00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.880
The Emmys only producers get the award.

237
00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:30.500
So like even when we won as a cast, we didn't get one.

238
00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:36.820
And this was really just like this great, amazing thing for us as a cast to be able to be celebrated

239
00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:37.380
like that.

240
00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:38.160
Yes.

241
00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:44.360
And I guess we were all in a very good mood because when we asked Andy Daly if he had

242
00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:48.580
any favorite memories from his time on the show, this is what he mentioned.

243
00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:55.280
It would be hard to say that I have one specific favorite memory, but I will tell you that the

244
00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:01.360
week that I was there, something was going on, like the entire cast was maybe going to the

245
00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:03.280
SAG Awards or something like that.

246
00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:07.440
And everybody was just really excited and in a good mood.

247
00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:13.940
And there was a lot of talk of dresses and tuxedos and hair and makeup getting done for

248
00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:14.900
some big event.

249
00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:22.640
And I was just really delighted by what a positive, happy mood everybody seemed to be in and what

250
00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:29.060
fun everybody in the regular cast seemed to be having every day, getting along very well

251
00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:29.760
with each other.

252
00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:35.800
You know, you end up on sets where there's different kinds of attitudes toward the work

253
00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:38.380
and the schedule and the co-workers.

254
00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:44.760
But this was one where everybody seemed so happy and to really realize how lucky they were

255
00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:50.380
