Can you, Jenna?

1117
00:52:34.090 --> 00:52:34.510
You know what?

1118
00:52:34.630 --> 00:52:35.830
Here's you know what I'm going to say.

1119
00:52:36.670 --> 00:52:38.570
I know if I can.

1120
00:52:38.650 --> 00:52:40.250
I know the answer to that question.

1121
00:52:40.570 --> 00:52:45.190
Wait, people are wrote in asking if you and I can tie a cherry stem into a knot.

1122
00:52:45.930 --> 00:52:50.170
Yes, that's a very, very personal question.

1123
00:52:50.490 --> 00:52:51.230
Is it?

1124
00:52:51.410 --> 00:52:52.350
It's very personal.

1125
00:52:52.610 --> 00:52:53.410
Like, who cares?

1126
00:52:53.670 --> 00:52:55.750
Who cares if you can tie one or not?

1127
00:52:55.990 --> 00:52:56.990
Like, seriously?

1128
00:52:57.510 --> 00:52:58.710
What does that mean?

1129
00:52:59.230 --> 00:52:59.750
Really?

1130
00:53:00.010 --> 00:53:00.450
Come on.

1131
00:53:01.230 --> 00:53:02.730
Well, where did this start?

1132
00:53:03.230 --> 00:53:06.850
I feel like this started in, like, a movie from the 80s or something.

1133
00:53:07.110 --> 00:53:07.970
Does anybody know?

1134
00:53:08.170 --> 00:53:09.850
I feel like this took off.

1135
00:53:09.990 --> 00:53:11.430
I remember this from my youth.

1136
00:53:11.430 --> 00:53:13.870
It was like this whole, like, thing.

1137
00:53:14.030 --> 00:53:16.870
Can you tie a cherry stem in your mouth?

1138
00:53:17.570 --> 00:53:20.810
I mean, so, weren't we all doing it?

1139
00:53:21.050 --> 00:53:23.170
Weren't we all tying cherry stems with our mouth?

1140
00:53:23.290 --> 00:53:23.870
We all tried.

1141
00:53:24.090 --> 00:53:27.610
Can this generation's youth tie cherry stems in their mouth?

1142
00:53:27.610 --> 00:53:28.370
We'll never know.

1143
00:53:28.590 --> 00:53:29.190
You know what?

1144
00:53:29.310 --> 00:53:30.630
Go on TikTok or something.

1145
00:53:30.750 --> 00:53:32.130
They're probably doing it on the talk.

1146
00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:33.330
Is that what they call it?

1147
00:53:33.470 --> 00:53:34.030
On the talk?

1148
00:53:34.330 --> 00:53:35.210
Get on the talk.

1149
00:53:35.470 --> 00:53:35.990
I'm sure they don't.

1150
00:53:36.250 --> 00:53:39.350
Now, Ben Franklin winks at Pam.

1151
00:53:39.610 --> 00:53:41.350
Hey, sussy wink.

1152
00:53:41.810 --> 00:53:43.030
Jenna, I have a question for you.

1153
00:53:43.590 --> 00:53:44.430
What is it?

1154
00:53:44.610 --> 00:53:48.950
Is an unsolicited wink ever a good thing?

1155
00:53:48.950 --> 00:53:51.130
Or is it always a douchey thing?

1156
00:53:52.650 --> 00:53:53.650
It's a douche move.

1157
00:53:53.650 --> 00:53:55.010
I think it's a little douchey.

1158
00:53:55.430 --> 00:54:00.010
I think, like, I mean, A, who even does it?

1159
00:54:01.230 --> 00:54:03.870
Who's like, who's like, hey, huh?

1160
00:54:05.470 --> 00:54:06.130
Yeah.

1161
00:54:07.090 --> 00:54:11.710
Well, it's interesting because we had someone write in.

1162
00:54:11.910 --> 00:54:17.890
Devin Crutchley said, did Pam secretly have a real crush on Ben Franklin?

1163
00:54:17.890 --> 00:54:24.870
Because she seemed to be playing into the banter, even after he becomes overly flirty with her.

1164
00:54:26.190 --> 00:54:29.110
And others start picking up on it and playing along.

1165
00:54:30.430 --> 00:54:31.730
Here's the thing, guys.

1166
00:54:31.970 --> 00:54:42.810
I was so utterly charmed by literally everything Andy Daly was doing as Ben Franklin that I am only ever, like, smiling at him.

1167
00:54:42.950 --> 00:54:44.890
It had to have been coming through in my performance.

1168
00:54:45.330 --> 00:54:54.110
And so I think that might have given you the impression that Pam secretly had a crush on Ben Franklin because I personally had a crush on Andy Daly's performance.

1169
00:54:54.110 --> 00:54:57.570
Well, I did not see it that way at all.

1170
00:54:57.710 --> 00:55:01.570
I just I just saw it as like, listen, none of us are having to work.

1171
00:55:01.970 --> 00:55:03.110
We're in this room together.

1172
00:55:03.370 --> 00:55:04.190
We're drinking champagne.

1173
00:55:05.010 --> 00:55:06.710
We're just like having a good time.

1174
00:55:07.110 --> 00:55:10.150
So, you know, that's how I saw it.

1175
00:55:10.930 --> 00:55:13.270
I mean, I didn't want Ben Franklin to leave.

1176
00:55:13.570 --> 00:55:16.170
So, you know, I was going to egg him on.

1177
00:55:16.530 --> 00:55:18.850
That was, I think, Pam's where Pam's coming from.

1178
00:55:19.010 --> 00:55:19.330
Keep him talking.

1179
00:55:19.330 --> 00:55:25.110
The longer he's talking, the less time I'm sitting there answering phones, looking at the back of Jim's head.

1180
00:55:25.430 --> 00:55:32.070
Wait, I just have to say, while this wink is happening in the conference room, in the warehouse Michael has been grilling steaks, they're done.

1181
00:55:32.070 --> 00:55:35.150
And he's like, hey, guys, who wants some man meat?

1182
00:55:35.430 --> 00:55:36.510
And Dwight goes, I do.

1183
00:55:36.870 --> 00:55:38.090
I want some man meat.

1184
00:55:38.250 --> 00:55:45.690
And then Creed picks the steak up with his hands because Stanley tries to cut it with a plastic fork and it breaks, which made me laugh so hard.

1185
00:55:45.830 --> 00:55:48.750
And then Creed just picks it up and starts eating it.

1186
00:55:49.330 --> 00:55:52.470
And we should have texted Creed to ask him how that steak tasted.

1187
00:55:52.690 --> 00:55:53.010
Oh, we should have.

1188
00:55:53.110 --> 00:55:56.270
I have to tell you guys, you know Creed lives down my street, right?

1189
00:55:56.330 --> 00:55:57.310
He's at the end of my street.

1190
00:55:57.750 --> 00:56:02.350
And he stopped by, front yard, social distance, hang out the other night.

1191
00:56:02.750 --> 00:56:11.710
And he said to us, guys, you have got to watch this show alone where they drop people off in the Arctic and then they have to survive.

1192
00:56:11.970 --> 00:56:13.370
It's like a survivalist show.

1193
00:56:13.950 --> 00:56:15.410
Jenna, I thought of you.

1194
00:56:16.370 --> 00:56:16.950
What?

1195
00:56:16.950 --> 00:56:20.670
What one man can do or whatever, another one can, you know.

1196
00:56:21.010 --> 00:56:22.030
No, don't whatever.

1197
00:56:22.290 --> 00:56:29.510
Don't whatever one of the greatest lines written by one of the great American playwrights, David Mamet, whatever.

1198
00:56:29.850 --> 00:56:32.730
What one man has done, another can do.

1199
00:56:32.950 --> 00:56:33.190
Okay.

1200
00:56:33.990 --> 00:56:36.750
Well, let me tell you, Jenna, you've got to watch this show.

1201
00:56:36.990 --> 00:56:39.230
It's so, so freaking good.

1202
00:56:39.710 --> 00:56:40.890
Creed has got us in it.

1203
00:56:41.090 --> 00:56:42.310
We are so invested.

1204
00:56:43.270 --> 00:56:46.430
It's the most intense survival series on television.

1205
00:56:47.550 --> 00:56:48.710
And it's in the Arctic.

1206
00:56:49.530 --> 00:56:50.250
I'm fascinated.

1207
00:56:50.530 --> 00:56:51.010
It's so good.

1208
00:56:51.170 --> 00:56:51.670
It's called Alone.

1209
00:56:52.910 --> 00:56:54.570
Do they have supplies?

1210
00:56:54.970 --> 00:56:57.430
They get to bring a few things, but they have to pick and choose.

1211
00:56:58.170 --> 00:57:01.930
And so like, oh, like one guy, you could bring a fire starter thing.

1212
00:57:01.930 --> 00:57:05.570
But one guy decided not to because he was like, I can make my own fire.

1213
00:57:05.870 --> 00:57:08.350
But then the first day he was like spent all day trying to make fire.

1214
00:57:08.470 --> 00:57:09.090
And he was like, damn it.

1215
00:57:09.170 --> 00:57:10.050
I should have brought that thing.

1216
00:57:10.250 --> 00:57:17.750
But anyway, when I saw Creed, who had just told me about the survivalist show, grab the steak with two hands and gnaw on it.

1217
00:57:18.670 --> 00:57:19.030
Yeah.

1218
00:57:19.250 --> 00:57:20.470
It just made me think of.

1219
00:57:20.910 --> 00:57:22.570
It all came full circle in your brain.

1220
00:57:22.590 --> 00:57:23.610
And you need to see it.

1221
00:57:23.690 --> 00:57:25.190
You love survivalist stuff.

1222
00:57:25.330 --> 00:57:25.750
I do.

1223
00:57:25.750 --> 00:57:35.390
OK, now we have to get into the very, very awkward scene that is Karen and Pam in the kitchen.

1224
00:57:35.730 --> 00:57:37.950
They're kind of joking about Ben Franklin.

1225
00:57:38.470 --> 00:57:43.230
I feel like they're both like doing a bit about how did he become a Ben Franklin impersonator.

1226
00:57:43.390 --> 00:57:54.530
And all of a sudden, Karen takes a sharp left turn and she says, hey, you know, I just want you to know Jim told me, you know, that you guys kissed.

1227
00:57:56.590 --> 00:57:59.690
And, you know, and Pam's like, what the what?

1228
00:58:00.950 --> 00:58:03.750
She is she's like, what is happening right now?

1229
00:58:03.970 --> 00:58:05.370
It's like her computer shut down.

1230
00:58:06.290 --> 00:58:06.770
Yes.

1231
00:58:07.230 --> 00:58:11.190
Now, pretty much none of what she says after this makes any sense.

1232
00:58:12.310 --> 00:58:12.790
No.

1233
00:58:13.050 --> 00:58:15.130
And OK, so we had someone write in.

1234
00:58:15.310 --> 00:58:17.490
Jackie Cortez said when Pam answers.

1235
00:58:18.170 --> 00:58:18.990
Oh, yeah.

1236
00:58:19.330 --> 00:58:20.190
To Karen.

1237
00:58:20.450 --> 00:58:22.610
When Karen asked if she still has feelings for Jim.

1238
00:58:23.090 --> 00:58:30.030
Do you think that was because Pam was feeling brave in the moment that she was like trying to say her real feelings in that moment?

1239
00:58:30.030 --> 00:58:32.370
And then she backs out when she sees Karen's face.

1240
00:58:32.530 --> 00:58:37.810
And also how much of this was scripted and how much of it was improvised?

1241
00:58:38.970 --> 00:58:44.150
OK, so first of all, this scene plays out exactly as it was scripted.

1242
00:58:44.810 --> 00:58:49.870
All of those like awkward jumps in dialogue were all scripted.

1243
00:58:50.150 --> 00:58:52.850
I just think it is such a great piece of writing.

1244
00:58:52.850 --> 00:59:00.490
And I remember I felt being challenged as an actor to make all those different turns that Pam has to make in the scene.

1245
00:59:00.490 --> 00:59:07.050
And so when she says, oh, yeah, that's because she heard the question.

1246
00:59:07.050 --> 00:59:09.050
You don't have feelings for Jim.

1247
00:59:09.250 --> 00:59:09.390
Right.

1248
00:59:09.670 --> 00:59:12.530
And she's like, oh, yeah, I don't have feelings.

1249
00:59:12.730 --> 00:59:14.090
But that's not what Karen said.

1250
00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:17.210
Karen said, you do you still have feelings for Jim?

1251
00:59:17.410 --> 00:59:18.590
